A||N||3||GT-maniac||Clutch going ?||||7||
Z||000000||GT-maniac||05-08-2000||11:39 PM||||My clutch pedal has to be pressed all the way to the floor to be engaged. Is this normal on fiero's ? Or is it a sign the clutch is going ? It's pretty stiff to engage too which I thought was strange as my other car also has a hydraulic clutch and it doesn't need near as much effort.||||reg||7
Z||000001||DXR_DAD||05-08-2000||11:47 PM||||Bent clutch pedal, Replace it with a steel one.<BR> <A HREF="http://www.v8archie.com/arch6.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.v8archie.com/arch6.htm</A> <p>[This message has been edited by DXR_DAD (edited 05-08-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||mrfiero||05-09-2000||02:33 AM||||It could also be bad hydraulics.  Crawl under the dash and remove the rubber end cap to the master cylinder.....just follow the rod from the pedal.  Look for/feel for any moisture.  If you get a hand full of fluid then your master is shot.<P>Do the same test on your slave.  Unbolt it from the transmission and remove the rubber boot & rod.  Look for fluid or any evidence of leakage.<P>I highly recommend replacing any questionable hydraulics with factory GM parts.  They cost a bit more, but the aftermarket slaves and masters are junk. <P>I have tried aftermarket parts many times and every time they have failed.   I have now "seen the light" and will only buy original GM hydraulics from now on.||||reg||1
Z||000003||ChadMan||05-09-2000||09:03 AM||||Clutch engagement right off the floor + high pedal effort = bad hydraulics.  You might get lucky and have it turn out to be a bent pedal, but I doubt it - that's just too cheap and easy to fix!<P>I have had good luck with NAPA replacement clutch master/slave cylinders.  The set I installed in my 84 lasted the life of the car, and the set I installed in my 88 Coupe still work great after 38K miles.||||reg||1
